02.Python/01.Basics-Python/33.Encapsulat.content:
33. Encapsulation Basics

“The goal of encapsulation is not secrecy — it is to draw a boundary between what a module promises and how it delivers on that promise.”

Prerequisites: 26.Classes and objects · 27.init, self · 28.Instance vs class vs static methods · 29.Attributes- instance vs class

What Is Encapsulation?
Encapsulation is one of the four pillars of object-oriented programming (alongside abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism). It has two inseparable ideas:

Bundling — data (attributes) and the operations on that data (methods) are packaged together inside a class.
Access control — the internal state of an object is hidden from the outside world; the outside world interacts only through a controlled interface.

The mental model: an object is a capsule. The interior is its private state. The surface is its public interface. You can press the buttons on the surface without knowing the machinery inside.
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│              BankAccount            │
│                                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  PRIVATE (hidden interior)  │    │
│  │  __balance = 1000.00        │    │
│  │  __transaction_log = [...]  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                     │
│  PUBLIC INTERFACE (the surface)     │
│  deposit(amount)                    │
│  withdraw(amount)                   │
│  get_balance()                      │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘

Without encapsulation, any external code could reach in and set account.__balance = -999999. The object’s internal invariants — the rules that must always hold — would be unenforceable.

Python’s Convention: Name Mangling, Not True Privacy
Unlike Java or C++, Python has no hard access enforcement. It uses naming conventions as a contract, relying on programmer discipline. This is a deliberate design choice: Python treats developers as adults.
There are three levels:

























ConventionSyntaxMeaningPublicself.nameFreely accessible from anywhereProtectedself._name”Internal use” — accessible but by convention onlyPrivateself.__nameName-mangled — hard to access accidentally

Public Attributes
No underscore. Open to everyone.
class Circle:
    def __init__(self, radius):
        self.radius = radius   # public

c = Circle(5)
print(c.radius)    # 5   — perfectly fine
c.radius = 10      # also fine

Protected Attributes — Single Underscore _
A single leading underscore is a social contract: “this is an implementation detail, please don’t touch it from outside.” Python does not enforce this in any way.
class Stack:
    def __init__(self):
        self._items = []     # protected by convention

    def push(self, item):
        self._items.append(item)

    def pop(self):
        if not self._items:
            raise IndexError(&quot;pop from empty stack&quot;)
        return self._items.pop()

s = Stack()
s.push(1)
print(s._items)   # [1]  — Python lets you do this
                  # but you are violating the convention
Use _name when building a library or framework to signal: “subclasses may use this, external users should not.”

Private Attributes — Double Underscore __ (Name Mangling)
A double leading underscore triggers name mangling. Python rewrites the attribute name to _ClassName__name at compile time.
class BankAccount:
    def __init__(self, balance):
        self.__balance = balance    # stored as _BankAccount__balance

    def deposit(self, amount):
        if amount &lt;= 0:
            raise ValueError(&quot;Deposit must be positive&quot;)
        self.__balance += amount

    def withdraw(self, amount):
        if amount &gt; self.__balance:
            raise ValueError(&quot;Insufficient funds&quot;)
        self.__balance -= amount

    def get_balance(self):
        return self.__balance


acc = BankAccount(1000)
acc.deposit(500)
acc.withdraw(200)
print(acc.get_balance())   # 1300

# Attempt direct access:
print(acc.__balance)       # AttributeError: &#039;BankAccount&#039; has no attribute &#039;__balance&#039;

# The real name after mangling:
print(acc._BankAccount__balance)   # 1300  — still accessible if you know the trick
Name mangling is not encryption. It is a speed bump to prevent accidental access and name collisions in inheritance hierarchies. A determined programmer can always reach it via _ClassName__name.

The Python philosophy: “We are all consenting adults here.” There is no true private. There is only clear signalling of intent.


Why Encapsulation Matters — Invariant Protection
The core benefit of encapsulation is protecting class invariants: conditions that must always be true for an object to be in a valid state.
class Temperature:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Invariant: temperature in Kelvin is always &gt;= 0.&quot;&quot;&quot;

    def __init__(self, kelvin):
        self.__kelvin = None
        self.set_kelvin(kelvin)

    def set_kelvin(self, value):
        if value &lt; 0:
            raise ValueError(f&quot;Temperature cannot be {value}K — below absolute zero.&quot;)
        self.__kelvin = value

    def get_kelvin(self):
        return self.__kelvin

    def get_celsius(self):
        return self.__kelvin - 273.15

    def get_fahrenheit(self):
        return self.__kelvin * 9/5 - 459.67


t = Temperature(300)
print(t.get_celsius())     # 26.85

t.set_kelvin(-10)          # ValueError: Temperature cannot be -10K — below absolute zero.
Without encapsulation, someone could do t.__kelvin = -273 and the invariant silently breaks.

The Pythonic Way — Properties
Raw getters and setters (get_balance(), set_balance()) are verbose and un-Pythonic. Python provides the @property decorator to give you getter/setter semantics with clean attribute-access syntax.
class Temperature:
    def __init__(self, kelvin):
        self._kelvin = None
        self.kelvin = kelvin     # triggers the setter

    @property
    def kelvin(self):
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Get temperature in Kelvin.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        return self._kelvin

    @kelvin.setter
    def kelvin(self, value):
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Set temperature — enforces Kelvin &gt;= 0.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        if value &lt; 0:
            raise ValueError(f&quot;Kelvin cannot be negative: {value}&quot;)
        self._kelvin = value

    @property
    def celsius(self):
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Read-only derived property.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        return self._kelvin - 273.15


t = Temperature(300)
print(t.kelvin)      # 300      — clean attribute syntax, calls getter
print(t.celsius)     # 26.85   — read-only

t.kelvin = 350       # calls setter, validates
t.kelvin = -5        # ValueError

t.celsius = 100      # AttributeError: can&#039;t set attribute — no setter defined
The anatomy of @property:
@property
def attr(self):           ← the getter; defines &#039;attr&#039; as a managed attribute
    return self._attr

@attr.setter
def attr(self, value):    ← the setter; called on assignment: obj.attr = x
    self._attr = value

@attr.deleter
def attr(self):           ← called on: del obj.attr
    del self._attr

This is the canonical Python approach to encapsulation. You start with a simple public attribute, and if you later need validation or computed logic, you retrofit a property without changing the public interface. This is the Uniform Access Principle in practice.

A Complete Example — Encapsulated Class with Properties
class Student:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    Invariants:
    - name must be a non-empty string
    - gpa must be in [0.0, 4.0]
    - credits must be a non-negative integer
    &quot;&quot;&quot;

    def __init__(self, name, gpa=0.0):
        self.name = name       # triggers setter
        self.gpa = gpa         # triggers setter
        self.__credits = 0     # private — external code shouldn&#039;t set this directly

    @property
    def name(self):
        return self._name

    @name.setter
    def name(self, value):
        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():
            raise ValueError(&quot;Name must be a non-empty string&quot;)
        self._name = value.strip()

    @property
    def gpa(self):
        return self._gpa

    @gpa.setter
    def gpa(self, value):
        if not (0.0 &lt;= value &lt;= 4.0):
            raise ValueError(f&quot;GPA must be in [0.0, 4.0], got {value}&quot;)
        self._gpa = round(value, 2)

    @property
    def credits(self):
        return self.__credits

    def complete_course(self, credit_hours):
        &quot;&quot;&quot;The only sanctioned way to increase credits.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        if credit_hours &lt;= 0:
            raise ValueError(&quot;Credit hours must be positive&quot;)
        self.__credits += credit_hours

    def __repr__(self):
        return f&quot;Student(name={self._name!r}, gpa={self._gpa}, credits={self.__credits})&quot;


s = Student(&quot;Alice&quot;, gpa=3.85)
s.complete_course(3)
s.complete_course(4)
print(s)               # Student(name=&#039;Alice&#039;, gpa=3.85, credits=7)

s.gpa = 5.0            # ValueError: GPA must be in [0.0, 4.0]
s.name = &quot;&quot;            # ValueError: Name must be a non-empty string

Encapsulation and Inheritance — Why __ Is Stronger Than _
Name mangling exists primarily to prevent accidental attribute name collisions when subclassing.
class Base:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__value = 10      # stored as _Base__value

class Child(Base):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.__value = 99      # stored as _Child__value — DIFFERENT attribute

c = Child()
print(c._Base__value)    # 10 — Base&#039;s copy, untouched
print(c._Child__value)   # 99 — Child&#039;s copy
With a single underscore, both self._value assignments would refer to the same attribute and the child would clobber the parent. Name mangling protects the parent’s internal state.

Encapsulation in AI/ML — Why It Matters Here
Encapsulation is not just good software hygiene — it is structurally embedded in the libraries you will use every day.
sklearn estimators are a textbook example:
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

model = LogisticRegression(C=1.0)
model.fit(X_train, y_train)

# After fitting, learned parameters are exposed as public attributes:
print(model.coef_)           # the weight vector w
print(model.intercept_)      # the bias b

# But the optimisation internals — solver state, iteration history — are hidden.
# You interact only through the interface: fit(), predict(), score()
The sklearn API contract (fit, transform, predict) is an encapsulated interface. You can swap LogisticRegression for SVC or RandomForestClassifier without changing a line of your pipeline code — because they all expose the same surface.
PyTorch nn.Module takes this further:
import torch.nn as nn

class MLP(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, input_dim, hidden_dim, output_dim):
        super().__init__()
        self.fc1 = nn.Linear(input_dim, hidden_dim)  # weights encapsulated inside Linear
        self.relu = nn.ReLU()
        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, output_dim)

    def forward(self, x):
        return self.fc2(self.relu(self.fc1(x)))
The weight tensors inside nn.Linear are managed internally. You access them via model.parameters() for the optimiser, not by reaching into the layer directly. The gradient computation (backward()) happens inside the module’s internal graph — you never touch it. The entire autograd engine is encapsulated behind loss.backward().
The practical lesson: when you design a custom nn.Module or a custom sklearn transformer, encapsulate your state. The buffers, running statistics, internal counters — keep them private. Expose only what a user of your component needs to interact with. This is what makes a component composable inside a larger Pipeline or nn.Sequential.

Summary — Decision Rules
Does external code need to read and write this attribute freely?
    → public:    self.name

Does it need read access but should not write directly?
    → @property (getter only, no setter)

Does it need write access with validation?
    → @property with getter + setter

Is it purely an internal implementation detail?
    → protected:  self._name   (convention)
    → private:    self.__name  (name mangling, stronger signal)

Is it a constant that defines the class, not an instance?
    → class attribute (see [[29.Attributes- instance vs class]])


Common Pitfalls
# PITFALL 1: Forgetting that __ is not truly private
class Secret:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__data = 42

s = Secret()
# s.__data            → AttributeError  (appears private)
# s._Secret__data     → 42              (actually accessible)

# PITFALL 2: Inconsistency — mixing raw attribute + property
class Bad:
    def __init__(self):
        self.x = 10          # public attribute set here

    @property
    def x(self):             # property with same name
        return self._x       # _x never set → AttributeError on init
    # Solution: use self._x in __init__, expose x as property

# CORRECT:
class Good:
    def __init__(self):
        self._x = 10         # use _x internally

    @property
    def x(self):
        return self._x

    @x.setter
    def x(self, value):
        self._x = value

# PITFALL 3: Over-encapsulating trivial state
# If an attribute has no invariant to protect and no logic on access,
# a plain public attribute is the right choice.
# Don&#039;t write getters/setters for everything — that&#039;s Java-brain.

Summary Table



































SyntaxStored AsAccessible FromUse Whenself.xxAnywhereDefault; no protection neededself._x_xAnywhere (convention)Internal/subclass use; signal intentself.__x_Class__xMangled name onlyPrevent inheritance collision; strong privacy signal@property_x (typically)Clean attribute syntaxValidation, computed values, read-only

Links
← 32.Dunder or magic methods → 34.iter() and next() → 30.Inheritance and method overriding — encapsulation interacts critically with inheritance → 57.First-class functions and closures — closures are a functional form of encapsulation

02.Python/01.Basics-Python/42.Raising-ex.content:
42. Raising Exceptions (raise)

“Raising an exception is an assertion. It says: this function’s contract has been violated, and I refuse to proceed on false premises. A function that silently accepts bad input is not being kind — it is deferring a lie.”


The Core Idea
A raise statement does one thing: it interrupts normal control flow and hands a signal — an exception object — up the call stack until something catches it or the program terminates.
This is not error-handling. That is try/except. raise is the other side: deliberately signalling that a precondition has been violated.
The mental model: every function has an implicit contract.
precondition  →  function executes  →  postcondition

When the precondition is violated, the function should not silently proceed toward a wrong postcondition. It should raise — loudly, early, with a precise message — and let the caller decide what to do.

Syntax: Three Forms
Form 1 — Raise a new exception
raise ExceptionType(&quot;message&quot;)
This is the standard form. Instantiate an exception class with a message string and raise it.
def sqrt(x):
    if x &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError(f&quot;sqrt requires a non-negative argument, got {x}&quot;)
    return x ** 0.5
Form 2 — Re-raise the current exception
raise
Used inside an except block. Re-raises the exception that is currently being handled, preserving its original traceback.
try:
    result = risky_operation()
except ValueError as e:
    log_error(e)    # do something — log, record, alert
    raise           # then let it propagate unchanged
Form 3 — Raise with explicit cause (raise ... from)
raise NewException(&quot;message&quot;) from original_exception
Chains exceptions explicitly. The new exception carries a __cause__ attribute pointing to the original. The traceback displays both.
try:
    value = int(user_input)
except ValueError as e:
    raise TypeError(f&quot;Expected an integer string, got {user_input!r}&quot;) from e
Output:
ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: &#039;hello&#039;

The above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:

TypeError: Expected an integer string, got &#039;hello&#039;


Raise is Not return
A raised exception does not return a value. It unwinds the call stack frame by frame, looking for an except clause that matches. If none is found, the program terminates and prints the traceback.
f() calls g() calls h()
                  ↓
              h() raises ValueError
                  ↓
         g() has no matching except → unwinds
                  ↓
    f() has except ValueError → caught here

Every frame between the raise and the except is unwound. Local variables in those frames are lost. This is why re-raising with raise (not raise e) is important — it preserves the original traceback all the way down to the actual source.

When to Raise: The Fail-Fast Principle
Raise early. Do not let bad data travel through your code until it produces a misleading error elsewhere.
Bad — silent propagation:
def train_step(model, X, y, lr):
    if lr &lt;= 0:
        lr = 0.001    # silently &quot;fix&quot; the problem

    # 50 lines later... the model converges to a strange solution
    # and you have no idea why
Good — fail fast:
def train_step(model, X, y, lr):
    if lr &lt;= 0:
        raise ValueError(
            f&quot;Learning rate must be strictly positive, got {lr}. &quot;
            f&quot;Typical values are in [1e-5, 1e-1].&quot;
        )
The rule: raise at the boundary where you first detect the violation, not deep inside where the consequence eventually surfaces.

What to Raise: Choosing the Right Exception
Always raise the most semantically accurate exception from the standard hierarchy. Never raise Exception directly when a subclass fits.













































ConditionRaiseArgument has the right type but wrong valueValueErrorArgument has the wrong type entirelyTypeErrorA required dict key is absentKeyErrorAn index is out of rangeIndexErrorAn attribute or method does not existAttributeErrorA file cannot be foundFileNotFoundErrorAn abstract method is not implementedNotImplementedErrorA general programming contract is violatedRuntimeErrorAnything that does not fit aboveCustom exception (see 43.Custom exception classes)
def set_activation(name):
    valid = {&quot;relu&quot;, &quot;sigmoid&quot;, &quot;tanh&quot;, &quot;gelu&quot;}
    if not isinstance(name, str):
        raise TypeError(f&quot;Activation name must be a str, got {type(name).__name__!r}&quot;)
    if name not in valid:
        raise ValueError(f&quot;Unknown activation {name!r}. Valid options: {sorted(valid)}&quot;)
Two checks, two different exception types, for two different kinds of violation.

Writing Good Exception Messages
An exception message is documentation that appears at the worst possible moment — when something is broken. Write it for the person debugging at 2am.
Minimum viable message:
raise ValueError(&quot;x must be positive&quot;)             # acceptable
raise ValueError(f&quot;x must be positive, got {x}&quot;)   # better
Complete message — what, why, what to do:
raise ValueError(
    f&quot;Learning rate {lr} is outside the valid range (0, 1). &quot;
    f&quot;Values above 0.1 often cause training instability. &quot;
    f&quot;Try lr=1e-3 as a starting point.&quot;
)
Always include the actual value. “x must be positive” without showing x = -7.3 forces the debugger to add a print statement and re-run. Show the value in the message.
# Bad
raise ValueError(&quot;Shape mismatch&quot;)

# Good
raise ValueError(
    f&quot;Weight matrix shape {W.shape} is incompatible with input shape {x.shape}. &quot;
    f&quot;Expected W to have {x.shape[-1]} columns, got {W.shape[-1]}.&quot;
)

raise Inside a Function: Guard Clauses
The guard clause pattern places all precondition checks at the top of the function before any work is done. The function’s main logic is then written assuming all preconditions hold.
def matrix_multiply(A, B):
    # Guard clauses — all preconditions up front
    if not isinstance(A, list) or not isinstance(B, list):
        raise TypeError(f&quot;Expected list inputs, got {type(A).__name__} and {type(B).__name__}&quot;)
    if len(A) == 0 or len(B) == 0:
        raise ValueError(&quot;Matrix arguments must be non-empty&quot;)
    if len(A[0]) != len(B):
        raise ValueError(
            f&quot;Incompatible dimensions: A has {len(A[0])} columns &quot;
            f&quot;but B has {len(B)} rows&quot;
        )

    # Main logic — written without defensive checks because guards already fired
    result = [[sum(A[i][k] * B[k][j] for k in range(len(B)))
               for j in range(len(B[0]))]
              for i in range(len(A))]
    return result
Contrast with the alternative: sprinkling if checks throughout the body. Guard clauses centralise all precondition logic, make the contract explicit, and keep the main logic clean.

Re-Raising: Preserving the Original Traceback
Inside an except block, bare raise re-raises the caught exception with its full original traceback intact.
def load_config(path):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        logger.error(f&quot;Config file not found: {path}&quot;)
        raise    # caller sees the original FileNotFoundError with its traceback
The critical difference — raise vs raise e:
try:
    risky()
except ValueError as e:
    raise e    # BAD: traceback starts here — original location is lost
    raise      # GOOD: traceback preserved back to the original raise site
raise e creates a new traceback starting at the current line. raise continues the original traceback. Always use bare raise when re-raising.

Exception Chaining: raise ... from
Python tracks two kinds of exception relationships:

























SyntaxAttribute setMeaningraise B from AB.__cause__ = AB was explicitly caused by AException during except A:B.__context__ = AB happened while handling A (implicit)raise B from NoneB.__cause__ = None, suppresses contextB replaces A completely
Explicit chaining — raise ... from
Use when a low-level exception should be re-expressed as a higher-level domain exception, but you want to preserve the original cause.
class ConfigError(Exception):
    pass

def load_config(path):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError as e:
        raise ConfigError(f&quot;Configuration file missing: {path}&quot;) from e
The traceback shows:
FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: &#039;config.json&#039;

The above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:

ConfigError: Configuration file missing: config.json

Both exceptions are visible. The caller sees ConfigError (the domain-level problem) and can trace it back to FileNotFoundError (the system-level cause).
Suppressing context — raise ... from None
Use when the original exception is an implementation detail that should not appear in the user-facing traceback.
def get_user(user_id):
    try:
        return db_query(f&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE id={user_id}&quot;)
    except DatabaseConnectionError as e:
        raise RuntimeError(&quot;User lookup failed — service temporarily unavailable&quot;) from None
The from None suppresses the DatabaseConnectionError from the traceback. The caller sees only a clean RuntimeError. Use this when the original exception contains internal details (SQL queries, internal service names) you do not want exposed.

Implicit Chaining — The Automatic Case
If an exception is raised while another is being handled, Python automatically sets __context__:
try:
    int(&quot;hello&quot;)
except ValueError:
    open(&quot;nonexistent.txt&quot;)    # raises FileNotFoundError
ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: &#039;hello&#039;

During handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:

FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: &#039;nonexistent.txt&#039;

Python shows both automatically. This is the “implicit chain” — no from keyword needed. It appears in tracebacks when your exception handler itself crashes.

Raising in Abstract Base Classes
The canonical use of raise NotImplementedError is enforcing method contracts on subclasses.
class Optimizer:
    def step(self, gradients):
        raise NotImplementedError(
            f&quot;{type(self).__name__} must implement step(). &quot;
            f&quot;See SGD or Adam for reference implementations.&quot;
        )

    def zero_grad(self):
        raise NotImplementedError(f&quot;{type(self).__name__} must implement zero_grad()&quot;)


class SGD(Optimizer):
    def __init__(self, lr=0.01):
        self.lr = lr

    def step(self, params, gradients):
        for p, g in zip(params, gradients):
            p -= self.lr * g

    def zero_grad(self):
        pass    # stateless optimizer — nothing to zero
NotImplementedError (not NotImplemented, which is a special singleton for rich comparisons) signals an incomplete inheritance — a subclass that forgot to implement a required method.

Raise in Assertions vs. Explicit Raise
Python has an assert statement:
assert x &gt; 0, f&quot;x must be positive, got {x}&quot;
This raises AssertionError if the condition is false. It is not a substitute for raise.






























assertraisePurposeDebugging, invariant checkingEnforcing contracts, validating inputDisabled bypython -O (optimise flag)Never — always runsException typeAlways AssertionErrorAny exception you chooseUse in productionNoYes
assert is for invariants that should never fail if your code is correct — internal consistency checks during development. raise is for conditions that can fail depending on external input and must be handled in production.
# WRONG — assert for user input
def set_learning_rate(lr):
    assert lr &gt; 0, &quot;lr must be positive&quot;   # disabled with -O; wrong exception type

# CORRECT — raise for user input
def set_learning_rate(lr):
    if lr &lt;= 0:
        raise ValueError(f&quot;lr must be positive, got {lr}&quot;)

# OK — assert for internal invariant
def _compute_gradient(loss, params):
    grad = autograd(loss, params)
    assert grad is not None, &quot;autograd returned None — this is a bug in _compute_gradient&quot;
    return grad

Raise Inside Context Managers
Raising inside a with block triggers __exit__ on the context manager before the exception propagates. This ensures cleanup always runs.
with open(&quot;data.csv&quot;) as f:
    line = f.readline()
    if not line:
        raise ValueError(&quot;Data file is empty — cannot proceed with training&quot;)
# File is closed here even though an exception was raised
If __exit__ returns True, the exception is suppressed. If it returns False or None, the exception continues propagating. Standard context managers (open, Lock, etc.) do not suppress exceptions.

Practical Patterns in ML Code
Input validation at API boundaries
import numpy as np

def fit(X, y, epochs=10, lr=0.01):
    # Type checks
    if not isinstance(X, np.ndarray):
        raise TypeError(f&quot;X must be a numpy array, got {type(X).__name__}&quot;)
    if not isinstance(y, np.ndarray):
        raise TypeError(f&quot;y must be a numpy array, got {type(y).__name__}&quot;)

    # Shape checks
    if X.ndim != 2:
        raise ValueError(f&quot;X must be 2-dimensional (n_samples, n_features), got shape {X.shape}&quot;)
    if y.ndim != 1:
        raise ValueError(f&quot;y must be 1-dimensional (n_samples,), got shape {y.shape}&quot;)
    if X.shape[0] != y.shape[0]:
        raise ValueError(
            f&quot;X and y must have the same number of samples. &quot;
            f&quot;Got X: {X.shape[0]}, y: {y.shape[0]}&quot;
        )

    # Value checks
    if epochs &lt;= 0:
        raise ValueError(f&quot;epochs must be a positive integer, got {epochs}&quot;)
    if not (0 &lt; lr &lt; 1):
        raise ValueError(f&quot;lr must be in (0, 1), got {lr}&quot;)

    # Main logic below — all preconditions guaranteed
    ...
Re-raising with context in training loops
def train_epoch(model, dataloader, optimizer):
    for batch_idx, (X, y) in enumerate(dataloader):
        try:
            loss = train_step(model, X, y)
        except RuntimeError as e:
            raise RuntimeError(
                f&quot;Training failed at batch {batch_idx}. &quot;
                f&quot;Input shape: {X.shape}, Target shape: {y.shape}. &quot;
                f&quot;Original error: {e}&quot;
            ) from e
Signalling unimplemented features cleanly
class Model:
    def predict(self, X):
        raise NotImplementedError

    def predict_proba(self, X):
        raise NotImplementedError(
            f&quot;{type(self).__name__} does not support probability estimates. &quot;
            f&quot;Use a probabilistic model such as LogisticRegression.&quot;
        )

    def save(self, path):
        raise NotImplementedError(
            f&quot;Serialization not yet implemented. &quot;
            f&quot;Track progress at github.com/org/repo/issues/42.&quot;
        )

The Full Raise Decision Tree
Something is wrong in my function.
           │
           ▼
Is this a violation of MY function&#039;s contract
(bad input, impossible state)?
    │                  │
   YES                 NO → handle it internally or let it propagate
    │
    ▼
Is there an accurate standard exception (ValueError,
TypeError, KeyError, etc.)?
    │                       │
   YES                      NO → raise a custom exception (note 43)
    │
    ▼
raise SpecificException(
    f&quot;What was wrong: {actual_value}. &quot;
    f&quot;What was expected: &lt;constraint&gt;. &quot;
    f&quot;What to do: &lt;hint&gt;.&quot;
)


Common Mistakes
Raising the base Exception class:
raise Exception(&quot;something went wrong&quot;)    # too vague — use a specific subclass
Swallowing and re-raising incorrectly:
except ValueError as e:
    raise ValueError(&quot;wrapped&quot;) from e   # creates a chain — fine if intentional
    raise ValueError(&quot;wrapped&quot;)          # implicit chain — traceback looks odd
    raise e                              # loses original traceback location
    raise                                # correct bare re-raise
Raising a string instead of an exception:
raise &quot;this is wrong&quot;    # TypeError in Python 3 — must raise an exception instance
Using assert for user-facing validation:
assert lr &gt; 0    # silently disabled with python -O; wrong exception type

Exercises
Exercise 1. Write a function safe_divide(a, b) that raises TypeError if either argument is not a number, raises ValueError if b is zero with the message &quot;Divisor must be non-zero&quot;, and returns a / b otherwise. Write three test calls that trigger each branch.
Exercise 2. What is the output of the following? Trace through it without running:
def f():
    try:
        raise ValueError(&quot;original&quot;)
    except ValueError as e:
        raise RuntimeError(&quot;wrapped&quot;) from e

try:
    f()
except RuntimeError as e:
    print(type(e).__name__)
    print(type(e.__cause__).__name__)
    print(e.__cause__)
Exercise 3. Explain the difference between:
raise RuntimeError(&quot;bad&quot;) from original_exception
raise RuntimeError(&quot;bad&quot;) from None
raise RuntimeError(&quot;bad&quot;)
What does each set on the new exception object? When would you use each?
Exercise 4. Write a Matrix class with a __getitem__ method that raises IndexError with a message that includes both the attempted index and the valid range. Then write a get(i, j, default=None) method that uses it internally but returns default instead of propagating the IndexError.
Exercise 5 (ML). You are writing a DataLoader class. Its __init__ takes a dataset, batch_size, and optional shuffle. Write the full set of guard clauses for __init__ using raise, covering: type of dataset, type and value of batch_size, type of shuffle, and the case where batch_size &gt; len(dataset).

Links

