43. Custom Exception Classes

“The standard exceptions are a vocabulary. Custom exceptions are the domain-specific dialect you invent when that vocabulary is not precise enough. A well-named exception is a compressed bug report — it tells you not just that something went wrong, but what kind of thing, in what layer, under what assumption.”


Why Custom Exceptions Exist
The standard library gives you ValueError, TypeError, KeyError, and a dozen others. These are sufficient for generic operations. They are not sufficient for domain reasoning.
Consider:
# Generic — tells you nothing about the domain
raise ValueError(&quot;invalid learning rate&quot;)

# Domain-specific — tells you exactly what failed and where
raise HyperparameterError(&quot;learning_rate&quot;, value=lr, constraint=&quot;must be in (0, 1)&quot;)
The second form lets you catch HyperparameterError specifically, write handlers only for configuration failures, and leave other ValueErrors to propagate. Without custom exceptions, every ValueError is the same — you cannot distinguish “bad user input” from “numerical domain error” from “malformed config file” without parsing the message string. That is fragile.
Custom exceptions give you:

Caller-side except clauses that match intent, not just type
Structured error data (not just a string message)
An exception hierarchy that mirrors your domain hierarchy
Self-documenting code — the exception name is part of the API


The Minimal Custom Exception
class ValidationError(Exception):
    pass
That is a complete, usable custom exception. It inherits everything from Exception — the message, the traceback, __str__, __repr__. Raise it exactly like a standard exception:
raise ValidationError(&quot;Input failed schema check&quot;)
Catch it specifically:
try:
    validate(data)
except ValidationError:
    # only runs for ValidationError, not for TypeError or ValueError
    ...
The pass body is not laziness. For many exceptions, the name alone is the information. class DataPipelineError(Exception): pass is a complete design decision — you are saying the exception carries a string message and nothing else.

The Exception Inheritance Contract
Every custom exception should inherit from Exception, not BaseException.
BaseException
└── Exception          ← inherit from here
    ├── ValueError
    ├── TypeError
    └── YourCustomError ← you are here

BaseException is the root of everything, including KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit. Code that does except Exception is a common pattern for “catch all application errors”. If your exception inherits from BaseException directly, it escapes that net silently. Never do this unless you are building something that deliberately should not be catchable by normal except Exception blocks — which is almost never.
# WRONG
class MyError(BaseException):   # escapes except Exception — almost certainly wrong
    pass

# CORRECT
class MyError(Exception):
    pass

Adding Structure: Custom __init__
When the exception needs to carry more than a string, define __init__.
class ShapeMismatchError(ValueError):
    def __init__(self, expected_shape, actual_shape, context=&quot;&quot;):
        self.expected_shape = expected_shape
        self.actual_shape   = actual_shape
        self.context        = context
        message = (
            f&quot;Shape mismatch: expected {expected_shape}, got {actual_shape}&quot;
            + (f&quot; ({context})&quot; if context else &quot;&quot;)
        )
        super().__init__(message)
Three things to notice:

Inherit from ValueError, not Exception — because a shape mismatch is semantically a value error. Callers catching ValueError will still catch this.
Store the structured data as attributes — the caller can access e.expected_shape and e.actual_shape programmatically, not by parsing a string.
Call super().__init__(message) — this sets args[0], which is what str(e) and repr(e) display. Without it, the string representation is empty.

try:
    result = model(X)
except ShapeMismatchError as e:
    print(f&quot;Expected: {e.expected_shape}&quot;)     # (32, 128)
    print(f&quot;Got:      {e.actual_shape}&quot;)       # (32, 64)
    print(str(e))    # Shape mismatch: expected (32, 128), got (32, 64) (in linear layer 3)

__str__ and __repr__
If the default message format from super().__init__ is insufficient, override __str__.
class ConfigError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, key, value, reason):
        self.key    = key
        self.value  = value
        self.reason = reason
        super().__init__(f&quot;Invalid config: {key}={value!r} — {reason}&quot;)

    def __str__(self):
        return (
            f&quot;ConfigError\n&quot;
            f&quot;  Key:    {self.key}\n&quot;
            f&quot;  Value:  {self.value!r}\n&quot;
            f&quot;  Reason: {self.reason}&quot;
        )
Note: __repr__ on exceptions is handled by the base class and generally does not need overriding. Override __str__ when you want a formatted, multi-line display.

Building a Domain Exception Hierarchy
For any non-trivial system, exceptions should form a tree that mirrors the domain structure. This lets callers choose how specific to be when catching.
# Root of the hierarchy — catch-all for the entire domain
class MLError(Exception):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Base exception for all errors in this ML library.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    pass


# Second tier — broad categories
class DataError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Errors in data loading, validation, or preprocessing.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    pass

class ModelError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Errors in model construction or configuration.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    pass

class TrainingError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Errors that occur during the training loop.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    pass


# Third tier — specific conditions
class ShapeMismatchError(DataError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Feature matrix shape does not match expected dimensions.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    def __init__(self, expected, actual, location=&quot;&quot;):
        self.expected = expected
        self.actual   = actual
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Shape mismatch at {location!r}: expected {expected}, got {actual}&quot;
            if location else
            f&quot;Shape mismatch: expected {expected}, got {actual}&quot;
        )

class MissingFeatureError(DataError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;A required feature column is absent from the dataset.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    def __init__(self, feature_name, available):
        self.feature_name = feature_name
        self.available    = available
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Required feature {feature_name!r} not found. &quot;
            f&quot;Available: {sorted(available)}&quot;
        )

class InvalidActivationError(ModelError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;An unknown or unsupported activation function was requested.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    VALID = {&quot;relu&quot;, &quot;sigmoid&quot;, &quot;tanh&quot;, &quot;gelu&quot;, &quot;selu&quot;}

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Unknown activation {name!r}. &quot;
            f&quot;Supported activations: {sorted(self.VALID)}&quot;
        )

class GradientExplosionError(TrainingError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Gradient norm exceeded the threshold, indicating unstable training.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    def __init__(self, norm, threshold, step):
        self.norm      = norm
        self.threshold = threshold
        self.step      = step
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Gradient explosion at step {step}: &quot;
            f&quot;norm={norm:.2f} exceeded threshold={threshold:.2f}&quot;
        )
How callers use this hierarchy:
# Catch everything from the library
except MLError as e:
    log_and_abort(e)

# Catch only data-related problems
except DataError as e:
    request_new_data(e)

# Catch only this specific condition
except ShapeMismatchError as e:
    reshape_input(e.expected)

# Catch two unrelated specific exceptions
except (InvalidActivationError, MissingFeatureError) as e:
    fix_config(e)
This is the core payoff of the hierarchy. The caller controls granularity.

Exceptions as Data Carriers
For failures in iterative processes — training loops, batch processing, pipeline stages — an exception that carries structured context is far more useful than a string.
class BatchProcessingError(TrainingError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Raised when a specific batch causes a failure during training.&quot;&quot;&quot;

    def __init__(self, batch_idx, epoch, cause, X_shape, y_shape):
        self.batch_idx = batch_idx
        self.epoch     = epoch
        self.cause     = cause     # the original exception
        self.X_shape   = X_shape
        self.y_shape   = y_shape

        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Batch {batch_idx} in epoch {epoch} failed. &quot;
            f&quot;Input shape: {X_shape}, Target shape: {y_shape}. &quot;
            f&quot;Cause: {type(cause).__name__}: {cause}&quot;
        )


# In the training loop:
for batch_idx, (X, y) in enumerate(dataloader):
    try:
        loss = train_step(model, X, y)
    except Exception as e:
        raise BatchProcessingError(
            batch_idx=batch_idx,
            epoch=current_epoch,
            cause=e,
            X_shape=X.shape,
            y_shape=y.shape,
        ) from e
The caller catching BatchProcessingError can log e.batch_idx, skip to the next epoch, or write the bad batch to disk for inspection — all without parsing a string.

The __init_subclass__ Pattern: Self-Registering Exceptions
For large libraries, you can build a registry that maps exception names to classes automatically.
class MLError(Exception):
    _registry = {}

    def __init_subclass__(cls, **kwargs):
        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)
        MLError._registry[cls.__name__] = cls

class DataError(MLError): pass
class ModelError(MLError): pass
class TrainingError(MLError): pass

# After class definitions:
MLError._registry
# {&#039;DataError&#039;: &lt;class &#039;DataError&#039;&gt;,
#  &#039;ModelError&#039;: &lt;class &#039;ModelError&#039;&gt;,
#  &#039;TrainingError&#039;: &lt;class &#039;TrainingError&#039;&gt;}
This enables deserialising exceptions from logs or network messages by name, without a manual lookup table. It is an advanced pattern — note it for later.

Organising Custom Exceptions in a Module
For any project beyond a single file, exceptions belong in their own module.
project/
├── exceptions.py       ← all custom exceptions live here
├── data/
│   ├── loader.py       ← from project.exceptions import DataError
│   └── pipeline.py
├── models/
│   └── base.py         ← from project.exceptions import ModelError
└── training/
    └── loop.py         ← from project.exceptions import TrainingError

exceptions.py:
&quot;&quot;&quot;
project.exceptions
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
All custom exceptions for the project, organised by domain layer.
Import from here, not from individual submodules.
&quot;&quot;&quot;

class MLError(Exception):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Base class for all project exceptions.&quot;&quot;&quot;

class DataError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Data loading, validation, and preprocessing failures.&quot;&quot;&quot;

class ModelError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Model construction and configuration failures.&quot;&quot;&quot;

class TrainingError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Training loop and optimisation failures.&quot;&quot;&quot;

class ShapeMismatchError(DataError):
    def __init__(self, expected, actual, location=&quot;&quot;):
        self.expected = expected
        self.actual   = actual
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Shape mismatch{f&#039; at {location!r}&#039; if location else &#039;&#039;}: &quot;
            f&quot;expected {expected}, got {actual}&quot;
        )

# ... rest of hierarchy
This single-module pattern means any file in the project can do:
from project.exceptions import ShapeMismatchError, TrainingError
And users of the library see a clean, documented exception surface.

__cause__, __context__, and Custom Exceptions
Custom exceptions participate in exception chaining exactly like standard ones.
class PipelineError(MLError):
    pass

# Translate a low-level OS error into a domain error
try:
    data = load_from_disk(path)
except OSError as e:
    raise PipelineError(f&quot;Failed to load dataset from {path}&quot;) from e
OSError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: &#039;data/train.csv&#039;

The above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:

PipelineError: Failed to load dataset from data/train.csv

The caller sees the domain-level PipelineError. The __cause__ attribute preserves the underlying OSError for debugging. The traceback shows both. This is the full exception chaining pattern from 42.Raising Exceptions (raise) applied to your own hierarchy.

Exceptions vs. Return Codes — The Design Choice
Some languages (C, Go) signal errors via return values. Python’s idiomatic approach is exceptions. The practical question is: when does a condition merit a custom exception vs. returning None or a sentinel value?
Use a custom exception when:

The condition represents a violated contract — the caller passed something invalid
Recovery requires knowledge of the failure type (which except gives you)
The failure should propagate unless explicitly handled
The error occurs deep in a call stack and returning it would pollute every intermediate function

Return a sentinel value (None, empty list, False) when:

Absence is a normal, expected outcome — not a failure
The caller always handles the “nothing found” case immediately
The operation is a query: “does this exist?” — False is the right answer

# Exception — contract violation: the user explicitly asked for a key that must exist
def get_required_config(key):
    if key not in config:
        raise ConfigError(f&quot;Required key {key!r} absent from configuration&quot;)
    return config[key]

# Return None — absence is normal: the user is probing, not asserting
def find_checkpoint(epoch):
    path = f&quot;checkpoints/epoch_{epoch}.pt&quot;
    return path if os.path.exists(path) else None

Summary: The Design Checklist
When designing a custom exception:

Where in the hierarchy? Inherit from the most specific standard exception that fits, or from your domain root. Never from BaseException.
What does it carry? Decide what structured data the caller will need — beyond the string message. Add those as __init__ parameters stored as attributes.
What is its name? The name should be a noun or noun-phrase describing the violated condition: ShapeMismatchError, MissingFeatureError, GradientExplosionError. Not BadInputError (too vague) or Error (meaningless).
Does super().__init__(message) get called? It must. Otherwise str(e) is empty.
Is it in exceptions.py If the project has more than one file, yes.


Full Working Example: A Mini ML Library
# exceptions.py

class MLError(Exception):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Base class for all library exceptions.&quot;&quot;&quot;


class DataError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Raised for data validation and preprocessing failures.&quot;&quot;&quot;


class ModelError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Raised for model construction and configuration failures.&quot;&quot;&quot;


class TrainingError(MLError):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Raised during training loop execution.&quot;&quot;&quot;


class ShapeMismatchError(DataError):
    def __init__(self, expected, actual, location=&quot;&quot;):
        self.expected = expected
        self.actual   = actual
        self.location = location
        msg = f&quot;Shape mismatch: expected {expected}, got {actual}&quot;
        if location:
            msg += f&quot; (at {location})&quot;
        super().__init__(msg)


class NaNLossError(TrainingError):
    def __init__(self, step, loss_value):
        self.step       = step
        self.loss_value = loss_value
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Loss became NaN at step {step}. &quot;
            f&quot;Check learning rate, gradient clipping, and input normalisation.&quot;
        )


class InvalidArchitectureError(ModelError):
    def __init__(self, layer_name, reason):
        self.layer_name = layer_name
        self.reason     = reason
        super().__init__(f&quot;Invalid layer {layer_name!r}: {reason}&quot;)


# ── model.py ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

from exceptions import ShapeMismatchError, NaNLossError, InvalidArchitectureError
import math

class LinearLayer:
    def __init__(self, in_features, out_features):
        if in_features &lt;= 0:
            raise InvalidArchitectureError(
                &quot;LinearLayer&quot;,
                f&quot;in_features must be positive, got {in_features}&quot;
            )
        if out_features &lt;= 0:
            raise InvalidArchitectureError(
                &quot;LinearLayer&quot;,
                f&quot;out_features must be positive, got {out_features}&quot;
            )
        self.W = [[0.0] * in_features for _ in range(out_features)]
        self.b = [0.0] * out_features
        self.in_features  = in_features
        self.out_features = out_features

    def forward(self, x):
        if len(x) != self.in_features:
            raise ShapeMismatchError(
                expected=(self.in_features,),
                actual=(len(x),),
                location=f&quot;LinearLayer(in={self.in_features}, out={self.out_features})&quot;
            )
        out = [
            sum(self.W[i][j] * x[j] for j in range(self.in_features)) + self.b[i]
            for i in range(self.out_features)
        ]
        if any(math.isnan(v) for v in out):
            raise NaNLossError(step=0, loss_value=float(&quot;nan&quot;))
        return out


# ── Usage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

layer = LinearLayer(4, 2)

try:
    output = layer.forward([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])  # only 3 values, expects 4
except ShapeMismatchError as e:
    print(f&quot;Shape error caught. Expected: {e.expected}, Got: {e.actual}&quot;)
    # Shape error caught. Expected: (4,), Got: (3,)

try:
    bad_layer = LinearLayer(-1, 2)
except InvalidArchitectureError as e:
    print(f&quot;Architecture error: {e}&quot;)
    # Architecture error: Invalid layer &#039;LinearLayer&#039;: in_features must be positive, got -1

Connection to AI, Machine Learning, and Deep Learning
Custom exceptions in ML are not optional polish — they are architectural decisions that determine how debuggable your system is under real failure conditions.
PyTorch’s Own Exception Design
PyTorch uses RuntimeError for most of its own failures — shape mismatches, device mismatches, missing gradients. This is a design choice you can improve upon in your own code by wrapping PyTorch errors in domain exceptions:
import torch
from exceptions import ShapeMismatchError

def safe_matmul(A, B):
    try:
        return A @ B
    except RuntimeError as e:
        if &quot;size mismatch&quot; in str(e):
            raise ShapeMismatchError(
                expected=f&quot;(..., {A.shape[-1]})&quot;,
                actual=str(B.shape),
                location=&quot;matmul&quot;
            ) from e
        raise   # re-raise anything else unchanged
Gradient Health Monitoring
class GradientError(TrainingError):
    pass

class VanishingGradientError(GradientError):
    def __init__(self, layer_name, norm, threshold=1e-7):
        self.layer_name = layer_name
        self.norm       = norm
        self.threshold  = threshold
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Vanishing gradient in {layer_name!r}: &quot;
            f&quot;norm={norm:.2e} &lt; threshold={threshold:.2e}. &quot;
            f&quot;Consider skip connections, batch normalisation, or a different initialisation.&quot;
        )

class ExplodingGradientError(GradientError):
    def __init__(self, layer_name, norm, threshold=1e3):
        self.layer_name = layer_name
        self.norm       = norm
        self.threshold  = threshold
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Exploding gradient in {layer_name!r}: &quot;
            f&quot;norm={norm:.2e} &gt; threshold={threshold:.2e}. &quot;
            f&quot;Apply gradient clipping: torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), max_norm=1.0)&quot;
        )

def check_gradients(model):
    for name, param in model.named_parameters():
        if param.grad is None:
            continue
        norm = param.grad.norm().item()
        if norm &lt; 1e-7:
            raise VanishingGradientError(name, norm)
        if norm &gt; 1e3:
            raise ExplodingGradientError(name, norm)
Configuration Validation
Config validation at startup is cheaper than debugging a run that crashed 8 hours in.
class ConfigError(MLError):
    def __init__(self, field, value, constraint):
        self.field      = field
        self.value      = value
        self.constraint = constraint
        super().__init__(
            f&quot;Invalid config field {field!r}={value!r}: {constraint}&quot;
        )

def validate_config(cfg):
    validations = [
        (&quot;learning_rate&quot;, cfg.get(&quot;learning_rate&quot;),
         lambda v: 0 &lt; v &lt; 1,
         &quot;must be in (0, 1)&quot;),
        (&quot;batch_size&quot;, cfg.get(&quot;batch_size&quot;),
         lambda v: isinstance(v, int) and v &gt; 0,
         &quot;must be a positive integer&quot;),
        (&quot;optimizer&quot;, cfg.get(&quot;optimizer&quot;),
         lambda v: v in {&quot;sgd&quot;, &quot;adam&quot;, &quot;adamw&quot;, &quot;rmsprop&quot;},
         &quot;must be one of sgd, adam, adamw, rmsprop&quot;),
    ]
    for field, value, check, constraint in validations:
        if value is None:
            raise ConfigError(field, None, &quot;is required but missing&quot;)
        if not check(value):
            raise ConfigError(field, value, constraint)
The Exception Hierarchy as an API Contract
When you publish a library, your exception hierarchy is part of the public API. Users write except YourLibrary.DataError in their code. If you rename or restructure exceptions in a patch release, you break their handlers silently. Treat exception class names with the same stability guarantees as function signatures.

Exercises
Exercise 1. Build a three-level exception hierarchy for a database access layer: a root DatabaseError, two second-tier classes ConnectionError and QueryError, and at least one specific third-tier class under each with a structured __init__. Write raise and catch examples for each level.
Exercise 2. What is wrong with the following, and what is the consequence?
class MyError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, code, message):
        self.code    = code
        self.message = message

e = MyError(404, &quot;not found&quot;)
print(str(e))
print(e.args)
Fix it.
Exercise 3. Implement a BoundedFloat class whose __init__ accepts value, low, high and raises a custom BoundsViolationError (inheriting from ValueError) if value is outside [low, high]. The exception should carry value, low, and high as attributes and display a message showing all three.
Exercise 4 (hierarchy reasoning). Given this hierarchy:
MLError
├── DataError
│   └── ShapeMismatchError
└── TrainingError
    └── NaNLossError

For each except clause below, list all exceptions it would catch:

except MLError
except DataError
except ShapeMismatchError
except (DataError, NaNLossError)

Exercise 5 (ML). Write a Validator class for an ML dataset with three checks: all feature arrays must be 2D, all label arrays must be 1D, and the number of samples must match. Each violation should raise a different custom exception (all inheriting from a common DatasetError). Write a validate(X, y) method that runs all three checks.

Links

41.Common exceptions (ValueError, TypeError, IndexError, KeyError etc) — the standard exceptions your custom ones inherit from
42.Raising Exceptions (raise) — how to raise custom exceptions and chain them
40.try except else finally — catching custom exceptions in handlers
26.Classes and objects — the class mechanics underlying exception definitions
30.Inheritance and method overriding — the inheritance chain that makes except DataError catch ShapeMismatchError
31.super() — why super().__init__(message) is required in every custom exception

02.Python/01.Basics-Python/58.Writing-an.content:
58. Writing and Applying Decorators (@decorator)

“A decorator is not syntax sugar for a clever trick. It is the formal mechanism by which Python lets you separate what a function does from how it is instrumented, wrapped, or transformed — without touching the function’s source code.”


Prerequisites
This note assumes you have fully absorbed:

57. First-Class Functions and Closures — decorators are built entirely on closures and higher-order functions
21. *args and **kwargs — the wrapper must be able to forward arbitrary arguments
55. Pass-by-Object-Reference Behavior — understanding what gets passed where

If any of those feel shaky, go back. A decorator written without understanding closures is a decorator you cannot debug.

Part I — What a Decorator Is
The Desugared Form
The @decorator syntax is exactly equivalent to:
@decorator
def func():
    pass

# is identical to:

def func():
    pass
func = decorator(func)
That is the entire definition. A decorator is a callable that receives a function and returns a callable. The @ syntax is assignment notation for that pattern.
def my_decorator(func):
    def wrapper():
        print(&quot;before&quot;)
        result = func()
        print(&quot;after&quot;)
        return result
    return wrapper

@my_decorator
def say_hello():
    print(&quot;hello&quot;)

say_hello()
# before
# hello
# after
say_hello is now wrapper. The original function object is captured inside wrapper’s closure.
After @my_decorator:

    say_hello ──────────────► wrapper (closure)
                                  │
                                  └── free variable: func ──► original say_hello body


Part II — The Correct Skeleton
The Universal Wrapper
Any decorator that wraps a function of unknown signature must use *args, **kwargs to forward arguments faithfully:
def decorator(func):
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        # --- pre-call logic ---
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        # --- post-call logic ---
        return result
    return wrapper
Without *args, **kwargs, the decorator only works on functions with the exact signature it expects — it breaks on everything else.
# WRONG — only works on zero-argument functions
def timer(func):
    def wrapper():                  # fixed signature
        ...
        return func()
    return wrapper

@timer
def train(model, epochs):           # TypeError at call time
    pass
# CORRECT
def timer(func):
    import time
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):   # forwards anything
        start = time.perf_counter()
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start
        print(f&quot;{func.__name__}: {elapsed:.4f}s&quot;)
        return result
    return wrapper

@timer
def train(model, epochs):
    pass

@timer
def predict(x):
    pass

functools.wraps — Non-Negotiable
Without it, the decorated function loses its identity:
def timer(func):
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        return func(*args, **kwargs)
    return wrapper

@timer
def train(model, epochs):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Train the model.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    pass

print(train.__name__)   # &#039;wrapper&#039;   ← WRONG
print(train.__doc__)    # None        ← WRONG
The decorated name is wrapper. Its docstring is None. Any framework that uses __name__ for routing, logging, or test discovery will break silently.
Always use functools.wraps:
import functools

def timer(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)          # copies __name__, __doc__, __module__,
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):   # __qualname__, __annotations__, __dict__
        import time
        start = time.perf_counter()
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        print(f&quot;{func.__name__}: {time.perf_counter() - start:.4f}s&quot;)
        return result
    return wrapper

@timer
def train(model, epochs):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Train the model.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    pass

print(train.__name__)   # &#039;train&#039;          ← correct
print(train.__doc__)    # &#039;Train the model.&#039; ← correct
functools.wraps is itself a decorator — a decorator that decorates wrapper to copy metadata from func. It also sets wrapper.__wrapped__ = func, giving you access to the original function:
original = train.__wrapped__   # the unwrapped function

Part III — Decorators with Arguments
A decorator with arguments needs one extra layer of nesting — a factory that receives the arguments and returns the actual decorator.
# No arguments:     @decorator
# With arguments:   @decorator(arg1, arg2)

# @decorator(arg) desugars to:
# func = decorator(arg)(func)
#         ─────────────  ────
#         returns decorator  applied to func
import functools

def repeat(n):                          # Layer 1: receives decorator arguments
    def decorator(func):                # Layer 2: receives the function
        @functools.wraps(func)
        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):   # Layer 3: the actual call wrapper
            for _ in range(n):
                result = func(*args, **kwargs)
            return result
        return wrapper
    return decorator

@repeat(3)
def greet(name):
    print(f&quot;Hello, {name}&quot;)

greet(&quot;Alice&quot;)
# Hello, Alice
# Hello, Alice
# Hello, Alice
The three-layer structure is mechanical and always the same:
repeat(3)          → returns decorator  (closes over n=3)
decorator(greet)   → returns wrapper    (closes over func=greet, n=3)
wrapper(...)       → calls greet(...)   (the actual execution)


Decorator That Works With or Without Arguments
Using functools.wraps and a check on whether func was passed directly:
import functools

def retry(func=None, *, times=3, exceptions=(Exception,)):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Can be used as @retry or @retry(times=5)&quot;&quot;&quot;
    if func is None:
        # Called with arguments: @retry(times=5)
        # Return the decorator
        return functools.partial(retry, times=times, exceptions=exceptions)

    # Called without arguments: @retry
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        last_exc = None
        for attempt in range(times):
            try:
                return func(*args, **kwargs)
            except exceptions as e:
                last_exc = e
                print(f&quot;Attempt {attempt + 1} failed: {e}&quot;)
        raise last_exc
    return wrapper

@retry
def unstable_call():
    import random
    if random.random() &lt; 0.7:
        raise ConnectionError(&quot;timeout&quot;)
    return &quot;success&quot;

@retry(times=5, exceptions=(ConnectionError, TimeoutError))
def api_call():
    pass

Part IV — Practical Decorator Patterns
1. Caching / Memoisation
import functools

def memoize(func):
    cache = {}                          # lives in the closure

    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args):
        if args not in cache:
            cache[args] = func(*args)
        return cache[args]
    return wrapper

@memoize
def fibonacci(n):
    if n &lt; 2:
        return n
    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)

print(fibonacci(50))   # instant — each subproblem computed once
Python provides this built-in as functools.lru_cache:
@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=128)
def fibonacci(n):
    if n &lt; 2: return n
    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)

# Inspect the cache
print(fibonacci.cache_info())
# CacheInfo(hits=48, misses=51, maxsize=128, currsize=51)

# Clear it
fibonacci.cache_clear()
functools.cache (Python 3.9+) is lru_cache(maxsize=None) — unbounded cache, slightly faster.

2. Timing and Profiling
import functools, time

def timer(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        start = time.perf_counter()
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start
        print(f&quot;[{func.__name__}] {elapsed*1000:.2f}ms&quot;)
        return result
    return wrapper

@timer
def matrix_multiply(A, B):
    return [[sum(A[i][k] * B[k][j] for k in range(len(B)))
             for j in range(len(B[0]))]
            for i in range(len(A))]

3. Input Validation
import functools

def validate_positive(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        for arg in args:
            if isinstance(arg, (int, float)) and arg &lt; 0:
                raise ValueError(f&quot;{func.__name__}: negative argument {arg}&quot;)
        return func(*args, **kwargs)
    return wrapper

@validate_positive
def sqrt(x):
    return x ** 0.5

sqrt(4)    # 2.0
sqrt(-1)   # ValueError: sqrt: negative argument -1

4. Logging
import functools, logging

logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)

def log_calls(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        logging.info(f&quot;CALL  {func.__name__}  args={args}  kwargs={kwargs}&quot;)
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        logging.info(f&quot;RETURN {func.__name__} → {result!r}&quot;)
        return result
    return wrapper

@log_calls
def load_dataset(path, split=&quot;train&quot;):
    pass

5. Access Control / Authentication
import functools

def require_auth(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(user, *args, **kwargs):
        if not user.get(&quot;authenticated&quot;):
            raise PermissionError(f&quot;User not authenticated for {func.__name__}&quot;)
        return func(user, *args, **kwargs)
    return wrapper

@require_auth
def get_model_weights(user, model_id):
    pass

6. Type Checking (Simplified)
import functools, inspect

def enforce_types(func):
    hints = func.__annotations__

    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        sig    = inspect.signature(func)
        bound  = sig.bind(*args, **kwargs)
        bound.apply_defaults()

        for param, val in bound.arguments.items():
            if param in hints and not isinstance(val, hints[param]):
                raise TypeError(
                    f&quot;{func.__name__}: {param} expected &quot;
                    f&quot;{hints[param].__name__}, got {type(val).__name__}&quot;
                )
        return func(*args, **kwargs)
    return wrapper

@enforce_types
def train(model: object, epochs: int, lr: float):
    pass

train(model, 10, 0.001)        # fine
train(model, &quot;ten&quot;, 0.001)     # TypeError: epochs expected int, got str

Part V — Stacking Decorators
Multiple decorators stack bottom-up. The decorator closest to def is applied first.
@A
@B
@C
def func():
    pass

# Equivalent to:
func = A(B(C(func)))
Application order (inside-out):
    C(func)       → func_c
    B(func_c)     → func_bc
    A(func_bc)    → func_abc   ← what the name &#039;func&#039; now points to

Call order (outside-in):
    func_abc called → A&#039;s wrapper runs
                      → B&#039;s wrapper runs
                        → C&#039;s wrapper runs
                          → original func runs
                        → C&#039;s wrapper continues
                      → B&#039;s wrapper continues
                    → A&#039;s wrapper continues

@log_calls
@timer
@validate_positive
def compute(x):
    return x ** 0.5

compute(9)
# INFO: CALL compute args=(9,) kwargs={}
# [compute] 0.003ms
# INFO: RETURN compute → 3.0
The order matters when decorators interact. Putting @timer outside @log_calls would time the logging overhead too.

Part VI — Class-Based Decorators
A class can be a decorator if it implements __call__. This is useful when the decorator needs to maintain richer state than a closure easily allows.
import functools

class CountCalls:
    def __init__(self, func):
        functools.update_wrapper(self, func)   # equivalent to @functools.wraps
        self.func  = func
        self.count = 0

    def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):
        self.count += 1
        print(f&quot;{self.func.__name__} called {self.count} time(s)&quot;)
        return self.func(*args, **kwargs)

@CountCalls
def train_step(batch):
    pass

train_step(batch1)   # train_step called 1 time(s)
train_step(batch2)   # train_step called 2 time(s)

print(train_step.count)   # 2  ← state directly on the object
The class-based form shines when you need methods to manipulate the decorator’s state, or when you want to expose that state as attributes.

Part VII — The ML and Deep Learning Connection
PyTorch @torch.no_grad() — A Decorator in Production
import torch

@torch.no_grad()
def evaluate(model, dataloader):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Run inference without building a computation graph.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    model.eval()
    total_correct = 0
    for X, y in dataloader:
        preds = model(X)
        total_correct += (preds.argmax(1) == y).sum().item()
    return total_correct / len(dataloader.dataset)
torch.no_grad() is a context manager and a decorator. When used as @torch.no_grad(), it wraps the function so that no gradient tape is built during its execution — saving memory and compute during evaluation loops. The implementation is exactly the class-based decorator pattern above.

Writing a @gpu_timer for Training Diagnostics
import functools, torch

def gpu_timer(func):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Time a function using CUDA events for accurate GPU timing.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        if torch.cuda.is_available():
            start = torch.cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)
            end   = torch.cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)
            start.record()
            result = func(*args, **kwargs)
            end.record()
            torch.cuda.synchronize()
            print(f&quot;[{func.__name__}] GPU time: {start.elapsed_time(end):.2f}ms&quot;)
        else:
            result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        return result
    return wrapper

@gpu_timer
def forward_pass(model, batch):
    return model(batch)

@property, @staticmethod, @classmethod
These are built-in decorators — the same mechanism, provided by Python itself. They are covered in depth in 59. @staticmethod, @classmethod, @property, but recognising them as decorators is important:
class NeuralNet:
    def __init__(self, layers):
        self._layers = layers
        self._param_count = sum(l.numel() for l in layers)

    @property
    def param_count(self):               # getter — called as net.param_count
        return self._param_count

    @staticmethod
    def xavier_init(shape):              # no self, no cls
        import math
        std = math.sqrt(2.0 / sum(shape))
        return [[std] * shape[1]] * shape[0]

    @classmethod
    def from_config(cls, config):        # receives class, not instance
        layers = [build_layer(c) for c in config[&quot;layers&quot;]]
        return cls(layers)
Each of @property, @staticmethod, @classmethod is a built-in that takes a function and returns a descriptor object — a different return type from a closure, but the same @ syntax, the same mechanism.

Skeleton Reference
# ── Decorator without arguments ──────────────────────────────
import functools

def decorator_name(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        # pre-call logic
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        # post-call logic
        return result
    return wrapper


# ── Decorator with arguments ─────────────────────────────────
def decorator_name(arg1, arg2):
    def decorator(func):
        @functools.wraps(func)
        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
            # use arg1, arg2 here
            result = func(*args, **kwargs)
            return result
        return wrapper
    return decorator


# ── Class-based decorator ─────────────────────────────────────
class DecoratorName:
    def __init__(self, func):
        functools.update_wrapper(self, func)
        self.func = func

    def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):
        # pre-call logic
        result = self.func(*args, **kwargs)
        # post-call logic
        return result

Common Mistakes — Checklist
□ Forgot *args, **kwargs in wrapper     → breaks on any non-zero-arg function
□ Forgot functools.wraps                → __name__, __doc__ are wrong; frameworks break
□ Forgot to return result               → decorated function always returns None
□ Forgot the extra layer for arguments  → @decorator(n) raises TypeError
□ Stacked decorators in wrong order     → outer decorator runs outermost at call time
□ Mutable state in closure (not cell)   → shared across all calls unexpectedly
□ Used func() instead of func(*args)    → silently drops all arguments


Summary
@decorator is syntactic sugar for: func = decorator(func)

The skeleton is always:
    outer function  → receives configuration (optional layer)
    decorator       → receives func, returns wrapper
    wrapper         → the new function; closes over func

functools.wraps copies __name__, __doc__, __module__ from func to wrapper.
It is mandatory. Without it, decorated functions lose their identity.

*args, **kwargs in wrapper makes the decorator signature-agnostic.
Without it, the decorator breaks on any function it wasn&#039;t written for.

Stacking: @A @B @C applies C first, then B, then A.
Call order is the reverse: A&#039;s wrapper runs first.

Class-based decorators use __call__ instead of a closure.
Prefer them when the decorator needs persistent state with rich access.

In ML: @torch.no_grad(), @property, @staticmethod, @classmethod are
all decorators. The mechanism is identical to anything you write yourself.


